would try to cover it up.

00:46:17.941 --> 00:46:19.443
The open attempt now
to throw people out

00:46:19.443 --> 00:46:22.212 align:start size:94% position:6%
of Hollywood for not going
along with the program.

00:46:22.212 --> 00:46:25.549
The amazing statement
that the Academy Awards

00:46:25.549 --> 00:46:28.485 align:start position:0%
is now going to be predetermined
based on what color

00:46:28.485 --> 00:46:31.188
are the leads in your film are
or what your staffing levels

00:46:31.188 --> 00:46:33.257
are of a particular
sexual orientation.

00:46:33.257 --> 00:46:34.792
It's absolutely insane.

00:46:34.792 --> 00:46:37.594
And that does have a
rather marked impact

00:46:37.594 --> 00:46:39.062
on how people think
about the world.

00:46:39.062 --> 00:46:40.864 align:start position:0%
Because obviously entertainment
is very emotionally driven.

00:46:40.864 --> 00:46:42.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
People tend to
think about politics

00:46:42.266 --> 00:46:44.034 align:start size:84% position:16%
on the back of their
emotional state.

00:46:44.034 --> 00:46:47.271 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so if the
entire entertainment
apparatus is taken over

00:46:47.271 --> 00:46:50.073
by the left and it is not in
fact appealing to the market,

00:46:50.073 --> 00:46:52.976 align:start size:97% position:3%
it is more acknowledging that
because it has a monopoly

00:46:52.976 --> 00:46:54.711 align:start size:97% position:3%
in the market, it doesn't
have to appeal to the market,

00:46:54.711 --> 00:46:56.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
whatever they
sell, you will buy.

00:46:56.613 --> 00:46:59.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
If they know that they're just
gonna continue doing this.

00:46:59.316 --> 00:47:01.752
- Do you think that we
can build better things

00:47:01.752 --> 00:47:03.654
and eventually get to
the place where Netflix

00:47:03.654 --> 00:47:06.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
won't be necessary and
Disney+ won't be necessary

00:47:06.023 --> 00:47:07.291
and everything else?

00:47:07.291 --> 00:47:08.525
- I mean, they have a
lot of legacy product.

00:47:08.525 --> 00:47:09.793
I mean the reason that
I subscribed to Disney+

00:47:09.793 --> 00:47:11.328
is not because I want to
see any of the new stuff

00:47:11.328 --> 00:47:13.163
it's because I want my kids to
be able to see the old stuff,

00:47:13.163 --> 00:47:14.598 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you just skip that
little warning at the front

00:47:14.598 --> 00:47:15.833
telling them that they're racist

00:47:15.833 --> 00:47:17.167 align:start size:81% position:19%
for watching "Lady
and the Tramp."

00:47:17.167 --> 00:47:19.369
But, it'll take time.

00:47:19.369 --> 00:47:21.104
I mean, Hollywood was
100 year enterprise.

00:47:21.104 --> 00:47:23.473 align:start size:84% position:16%
So this is the bottom
line of all this,

00:47:23.473 --> 00:47:24.708
this stuff takes time.

00:47:24.708 --> 00:47:25.676
Conservatives are always looking

00:47:25.676 --> 00:47:27.277
for like a turnkey solution,

00:47:27.277 --> 00:47:28.478
'cause that's always
what we do in business.

00:47:28.478 --> 00:47:30.247
But most of this stuff is stuff

00:47:30.247 --> 00:47:33.650 align:start size:97% position:3%
that members of the left
have spent decades building.

00:47:33.650 --> 00:47:35.152
- Right.

00:47:35.152 --> 00:47:37.354
And the irony is it's like,
man, we need new stories.

00:47:37.354 --> 00:47:38.589 align:start size:84% position:16%
We've discussed this
a million times.

00:47:38.589 --> 00:47:40.424 align:start size:81% position:19%
But "Star Wars"
is basically dead.

00:47:40.424 --> 00:47:41.658
They killed it now.

00:47:41.658 --> 00:47:43.527
The Marvel movies
are basically dead.

00:47:43.527 --> 00:47:44.995
They killed them now.

00:47:44.995 --> 00:47:46.964
It's like, we just need new
stories if nothing else.

00:47:46.964 --> 00:47:49.499
And then we gotta figure out
the way to get them out there

00:47:49.499 --> 00:47:51.034 align:start size:81% position:19%
and the way to
put them together.

00:47:51.034 --> 00:47:52.703
But we, but it's actually,
it starts with the story.

00:47:52.703 --> 00:47:53.937
- It does.

00:47:53.937 --> 00:47:55.138
And it also starts with
people who are willing

00:47:55.138 --> 00:47:56.440
to actually put money
into these things.

00:47:56.440 --> 00:47:57.941
And that's a risky endeavor.

00:47:57.941 --> 00:47:59.409
I mean, Hollywood's risky.

00:47:59.409 --> 00:48:01.311 align:start position:0%
And when business people on
the right think about business,

00:48:01.311 --> 00:48:02.546
they don't think about Hollywood

00:48:02.546 --> 00:48:05.115
because Hollywood
is such a risk.

00:48:05.115 --> 00:48:06.817
And then the last person who
actually invested in a movie

00:48:06.817 --> 00:48:09.353
and made money from it in
Hollywood doesn't exist.

00:48:10.520 --> 00:48:11.989 align:start size:84% position:16%
Hollywood accounting
has its own term.

00:48:11.989 --> 00:48:13.190
Right?

00:48:13.190 --> 00:48:14.725 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, so it's
going to take people

00:48:14.725 --> 00:48:16.159
who are really committed to
the cause to actually start

00:48:16.159 --> 00:48:18.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
making entertainment
that appeals to a broader

00:48:18.362 --> 00:48:19.329
spectrum of people.

00:48:20.664 --> 00:48:22.065 align:start position:0%
- And by the way, that's
exactly what you guys are doing.

00:48:22.065 --> 00:48:23.066
Right?

00:48:23.066 --> 00:48:24.234
- We're doing our best, yeah.

00:48:24.234 --> 00:48:25.168 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean that's definitely
what we're focused

00:48:25.168 --> 00:48:26.403
on at the moment, for sure.

00:48:26.403 --> 00:48:27.671
- Yeah.

00:48:27.671 --> 00:48:28.906
All right, we talked
about chapter seven,

00:48:28.906 --> 00:48:29.840 align:start size:88% position:13%
but anything you want
to add on the fake news?

00:48:29.840 --> 00:48:31.074
I mean, is there anyone...

00:48:31.074 --> 00:48:32.910
Like, do you turn on
anyone at this point?

00:48:32.910 --> 00:48:35.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
I thought Jake Tapper was
sort of the last hope at CNN.

00:48:35.779 --> 00:48:37.214
To me, he's gone in all the way.

00:48:37.214 --> 00:48:40.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like, is there anyone in
mainstream media at this point

00:48:40.183 --> 00:48:43.387 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you're like, "Ah, you're
not completely horrible."

00:48:43.387 --> 00:48:44.454
Which is all I would ask.

00:48:44.454 --> 00:48:45.589
They can be mostly horrible,

00:48:45.589 --> 00:48:46.723 align:start size:84% position:16%
but just not
completely horrible.

00:48:46.723 --> 00:48:47.958
- Yeah.

00:48:47.958 --> 00:48:49.359
I mean the way that I
tend to characterize

00:48:49.359 --> 00:48:51.128 align:start size:97% position:3%
this is there are no
longer journalists I watch,

00:48:51.128 --> 00:48:53.864 align:start size:97% position:3%
there are acts of journalism
that I will watch.

00:48:53.864 --> 00:48:55.565
But what I mean by that is
that I don't actually believe

00:48:55.565 --> 00:48:57.401
in the idea that journalists
are sort of a separate class

00:48:57.401 --> 00:48:58.835
of higher human being.

00:48:58.835 --> 00:49:00.737 align:start position:0%
Because I don't think they
very often follow the processes

00:49:00.737 --> 00:49:02.406
of journalism they
themselves espouse.

00:49:02.406 --> 00:49:04.875
And so I'm mostly looking
for people who commit acts

00:49:04.875 --> 00:49:07.244 align:start position:0%
of journalism, and that's really
what Twitter is for, right?

00:49:07.244 --> 00:49:08.745
Jake asks a good question.

00:49:08.745 --> 00:49:10.147
You're like, "Okay, well
that was a good question."

00:49:10.147 --> 00:49:11.715 align:start position:0%
It's not like I all of a
sudden, you know, buy hook, line

00:49:11.715 --> 00:49:14.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
and sinker everything
that Jake Tapper tells me.

00:49:14.251 --> 00:49:15.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
But when somebody commits
an act of journalism,

00:49:15.585 --> 00:49:16.853
then they deserve praise.

00:49:16.853 --> 00:49:19.423
And when they commit an
act of a journalisming,

00:49:19.423 --> 00:49:20.757
then they certainly do not.

00:49:22.559 --> 00:49:25.262
- Chapter eight, this seems
like the right way to end

00:49:25.262 --> 00:49:26.363
this before we get
to the conclusion,

00:49:26.363 --> 00:49:28.265
"Unfriending Americans."

00:49:28.265 --> 00:49:30.434
We shouldn't hate everybody,
Ben, was that what your tweet

00:49:30.434 --> 00:49:31.568 align:start size:81% position:19%
from earlier today
was all about?

00:49:31.568 --> 00:49:32.602
- Yeah.

00:49:32.602 --> 00:49:34.071
I mean, it's pretty easy.

00:49:34.071 --> 00:49:35.305
It was amazing, I was talking
to some people on the left

00:49:35.305 --> 00:49:37.541 align:start size:81% position:19%
about polarization
a few weeks ago

00:49:37.541 --> 00:49:39.810 align:start size:84% position:16%
and people were
throwing around ideas,

00:49:39.810 --> 00:49:41.078 align:start size:84% position:16%
many of which I
thought were silly,

00:49:41.078 --> 00:49:42.279
like get rid of gerrymandering.

00:49:42.279 --> 00:49:43.547 align:start size:84% position:16%
I'm like, "Well, we've
had gerrymandering

00:49:43.547 --> 00:49:45.082
since the beginning
and I'm pretty sure

00:49:45.082 --> 00:49:47.718
that our polarization is not
because of gerrymandering."

00:49:47.718 --> 00:49:49.086
And I said, I have
a very simple thing

00:49:49.086 --> 00:49:50.554
that all of us could do today.

00:49:50.554 --> 00:49:53.023 align:start size:97% position:3%
Go online today, on Twitter,
find somebody who voted

00:49:53.023 --> 00:49:55.325 align:start size:84% position:16%
for somebody that
you didn't vote for.

00:49:55.325 --> 00:49:56.159
Right?

00:49:56.159 --> 00:49:57.327
Whether, say Trump.

00:49:57.327 --> 00:49:58.762 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then say that
person's a human being.

00:49:58.762 --> 00:49:59.596
That's all.

00:49:59.596 --> 00:50:00.430
That's the whole thing.

00:50:00.430 --> 00:50:01.365
That's the whole tweet.

00:50:01.365 --> 00:50:03.100
And nobody took me up on it.

00:50:03.100 --> 00:50:04.835 align:start size:97% position:3%
Very few people on the left
I saw were taking me up

00:50:04.835 --> 00:50:06.336
on this today.

00:50:06.336 --> 00:50:07.838
I was seeing a lot of people
on the right were tweeting out

00:50:07.838 --> 00:50:09.706
here are a bunch of people on
the left who I am interested

00:50:09.706 --> 00:50:12.175
in reading and respect and
disagree with but I find

00:50:12.175 --> 00:50:13.577
them an interesting person.

00:50:13.577 --> 00:50:15.779 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I've yet to see anybody
from the left tweet,

00:50:15.779 --> 00:50:17.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
in fact the only thing
I saw was somebody

00:50:17.214 --> 00:50:18.815 align:start size:97% position:3%
from Media Matters tweeting
out my bad old tweets.

00:50:18.815 --> 00:50:20.884
Right, to demonstrate
that I am not worthy

00:50:20.884 --> 00:50:22.119
of being treated that way.

00:50:22.119 --> 00:50:25.389 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because that's sort
of a radical asymmetry

00:50:25.389 --> 00:50:27.057
where people on the right
tend to consider people

00:50:27.057 --> 00:50:28.525
on the left mistaken,
but people on the left

00:50:28.525 --> 00:50:30.327 align:start size:88% position:13%
tend to consider people
in the right evil.

00:50:30.327 --> 00:50:33.430
That radical asymmetry is not
gonna be cured anytime soon.

00:50:33.430 --> 00:50:37.167 align:start size:84% position:16%
- At what point does
that radical asymmetry
no longer work?

00:50:37.167 --> 00:50:39.736 align:start position:0%
Like when these people lie
over and over, so when they lie

00:50:39.736 --> 00:50:42.773
about Kavanaugh and they
lie about CRT and they lie

00:50:42.773 --> 00:50:44.608
about election bills, and
we know they're lying,

00:50:44.608 --> 00:50:45.842
and they know we're lying,

00:50:45.842 --> 00:50:47.110
and they know we
know they're lying.

00:50:47.110 --> 00:50:47.944
They continue to lie.

00:50:47.944 --> 00:50:48.912
The whole thing.

00:50:48.912 --> 00:50:51.048
At what point is it just evil?

00:50:51.048 --> 00:50:52.616
Like really?

00:50:52.616 --> 00:50:53.884
And I don't wanna get, you
know me, I don't wanna get

00:50:53.884 --> 00:50:55.485
to that hyper partisan
place but at what point

00:50:55.485 --> 00:50:57.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
do we just have to
call it for what it is?

00:50:57.220 --> 00:50:58.989
Not to say there can't be
a couple of good people

00:50:58.989 --> 00:51:00.557
who have different
political beliefs,

00:51:00.557 --> 00:51:03.493
but that the machinery of
it is just endless lying.

00:51:03.493 --> 00:51:05.762 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Well, I mean, I think
that as I say in the book,

00:51:05.762 --> 00:51:07.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
the institutions
themselves have become

00:51:07.364 --> 00:51:09.132 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
these sorts of
machines in many cases.

00:51:09.132 --> 00:51:11.201 line:0%
And so there are only two
choices, you can try to fix

00:51:11.201 --> 00:51:14.471 line:0%
the machinery from within, or
you can just pose alternatives

00:51:14.471 --> 00:51:16.540 align:start size:84% position:16%
to the machinery
that out-compete them
and destroy them.

00:51:16.540 --> 00:51:18.642
And when it comes to the
media I'm fully convinced

00:51:18.642 --> 00:51:20.077 align:start size:88% position:13%
that there is no fixing
the New York Times,

00:51:20.077 --> 00:51:22.245 align:start size:97% position:3%
there's no fixing CNN,
there's no fixing, you know,

00:51:22.245 --> 00:51:23.647
The Washington Post.

00:51:23.647 --> 00:51:24.614
You need to compete from outside

00:51:24.614 --> 00:51:25.849
and you need to take them down.

00:51:25.849 --> 00:51:28.485
And I think that that is
a worthwhile endeavor.

00:51:28.485 --> 00:51:32.122
- Shapiro, we both have one
minute remaining, 60 seconds,

00:51:32.122 --> 00:51:34.958
I want fastest Ben Shapiro,
white pill stuff right now.

00:51:34.958 --> 00:51:36.626
Like how do people feel
good about all this?

00:51:36.626 --> 00:51:39.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's the direction I'm
trying to go with this show.

00:51:39.062 --> 00:51:40.497
And I know that's what
you're trying to do.

00:51:40.497 --> 00:51:42.065 align:start size:97% position:3%
- I mean, I think the reason
that you can feel good

00:51:42.065 --> 00:51:44.468
is because we have not
started to push back yet.

00:51:44.468 --> 00:51:46.002
And I think a lot of people
on the right thought Trump

00:51:46.002 --> 00:51:47.270
was the pushback.

00:51:47.270 --> 00:51:48.572
No Trump was, you
went, you voted.

00:51:48.572 --> 00:51:50.006
Now you have to actually
push back in the places

00:51:50.006 --> 00:51:51.274
that matters in your own life.

00:51:51.274 --> 00:51:52.542
You have to push back
on the school board,

00:51:52.542 --> 00:51:53.810
you have to push back
at your corporation.

00:51:53.810 --> 00:51:55.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
And believe you, me,
more people agree

00:51:55.412 --> 00:51:57.180
with you than disagree
with you at these places.

00:51:57.180 --> 00:51:59.316 align:start size:97% position:3%
Because you're not even
taking radical points of view.

00:51:59.316 --> 00:52:02.752 align:start size:94% position:6%
When you say, I don't want
my business telling me

00:52:02.752 --> 00:52:04.454
how to think about
how I'm a racist.

00:52:04.454 --> 00:52:06.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't want my kid taught
that stuff in school.

00:52:06.823 --> 00:52:08.658
I don't think that I,
as a religious person,

00:52:08.658 --> 00:52:11.628 align:start size:97% position:3%
ought to be forced into
participating in activities

00:52:11.628 --> 00:52:12.562
I don't like.

00:52:12.562 --> 00:52:13.797
Most Americans agree with you.

00:52:13.797 --> 00:52:15.098
Which means that there
is fertile ground here.

00:52:15.098 --> 00:52:16.099
The left has pushed so far.

00:52:16.099 --> 00:52:17.300
I think they've pushed too far

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:19.302
and I think the blow
back is only beginning.

00:52:19.302 --> 00:52:21.404
- Shapiro, you still got it.

00:52:21.404 --> 00:52:22.939
- I appreciate that.

00:52:22.939 --> 00:52:24.141
- And I think if you stick
with it, you're gonna make

00:52:24.141 --> 00:52:25.542
